 "uwf", "mitre_references": "[]", "provenance_ref": "corpus/uwf/uwf_hist_014.json", "source_paths": "[\"data/logs/UWF-ZeekDataFall22-parquet/2022-01-02%20-%202022-01-09/part-00000-745e350a-da9e-4619-bd52-8cc23bb41ad5-c000.snappy.parquet\"]", "original_id": "uwf_des_sigiloso_0011", "vector_id": "rag_enriquecido_30_v1:uwf_hist_014", "partition": "desarrollo", "condition": "fondo_normal_original", "observed_patterns": "{\"connection_state_counts\":{\"OTH\":1,\"S0\":275,\"SF\":24},\"destination_ports_top5\":[[\"53\",296],[\"137\",1],[\"134\",1],[\"138\",1],[\"547\",1]],\"distinct_destination_ips\":11,\"distinct_source_ips\":8,\"protocol_counts\":{\"icmp\":1,\"udp\":299},\"service_counts\":{\"-\":3,\"dns\":297}}"}, "similitud": 0.997}, {"texto": "v2|lineas=300 ips=14 servicios=[dns:297] eventos=[evento_critico:274,evento_normal:26] eventos_totales=300 decoders=[csv:300] productos=[Zeek:300] severidades=[] categorias=[network:300] cobertura=1.0", "meta": {"source_hashes": "[\"18eda10d9ee3816380d448b86c440d55dc337ff4df5d8ac568cf7173ae61af60\"]", "fragment_sha256": "83efbd2b779f1b7ca983c7c0d493d5f71a6c4232c1dfff85caa06b5c30be3fa0", "record_identifiers_sha256": "b544e21b2ec6f5a95e0075642dff55df7c9895142a413c814793a089d8998dfc", "mitigacion": "No aplica: comportamiento normal según etiquetas originales.", "features": "{\"decoder_distribution\":{\"csv\":300},\"event_categories\":{\"network\":300},\"parsing\":{\"coverage\":1.0,\"fallback_events\":0,\"parsed_events\":300},\"product_distribution\":{\"Zeek\":300},\"severity_distribution\":{},\"total_eventos\":300}", "records_count": 300, "observed_patterns": "{\"connection_state_counts\":{\"S0\":274,\"SF\":26},\"destination_ports_top5\":[[\"53\",294],[\"137\",3],[\"138\",2],[\"547\",1]],\"distinct_destination_ips\":7,\"distinct_source_ips\":7,\"protocol_counts\":{\"udp\":300},\"service_counts\":{\"-\":3,\"dns\":297}}", "provenance_ref": "corpus/uwf/uwf_hist_013.json", "partition": "desarrollo", "condition": "fondo_normal_original", "original_label": "False", "source_paths": "[\"data/logs/UWF-ZeekDataFall22-parquet/2021-12-26%20-%202022-01-02/part-00000-94d13437-ae00-4a8c-9f38-edd0196cfdee-c000.snappy.parquet\"]", "corpus_version": "rag_enriquecido_30_v1", "mitre_references": "[]", "historical_case_id": "uwf_hist_013", "knowledge_origin": "historical_development", "original_id": "uwf_des_sigiloso_0010", "clasificacion": "normal", "dataset": "uwf", "descripcion": "UWF: 300 registros originales; referencia normal (False). Observado: {\"connection_state_counts\":{\"S0\":274,\"SF\":26},\"destination_ports_top5\":[[\"53\",294],[\"137\",3],[\"138\",2],[\"547\",1]],\"distinct_destination_ips\":7,\"distinct_source_ips\":7,\"protocol_counts\":{\"udp\":300},\"service_counts\":{\"-\":3,\"dns\":297}}", "vector_id": "rag_enriquecido_30_v1:uwf_hist_013"}, "similitud": 0.997}], "tecnicas_mitre": [{"texto": "DNS. Adversaries may communicate using the Domain Name System (DNS) application layer protocol to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the client and server. \n\nThe DNS protocol serves an administrative function in computer networking and thus may be very common in environments. DNS traffic may also be allowed even before network authentication is completed. DNS packets contain many fields and headers in which data can be concealed. Often known as DNS tunneling, adversaries may abuse DNS to communicate with systems under their control within a victim network while also mimicking normal, expected traffic.(Citation: PAN DNS Tunneling)(", "meta": {"name": "DNS", "technique_id": "T1071.004", "url": "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/004", "tactics": "command-and-control", "is_subtechnique": true}, "similitud": 0.434}, {"texto": "Domain Generation Algorithms. Adversaries may make use of Domain Generation Algorithms (DGAs) to dynamically identify a destination domain for command and control traffic rather than relying on a list of static IP addresses or domains. This has the advantage of making it much harder for defenders to block, track, or take over the command and control channel, as there potentially could be thousands of domains that malware can check for instructions.(Citation: Cybereason Dissecting DGAs)(Citation: Cisco Umbrella DGA)(Citation: Unit 42 DGA Feb 2019)\n\nDGAs can take the form of apparently random or “gibberish” strings (ex: istgmxdejdnxuyla.ru) when they construct domain names by generating each letter. Alternatively, some DGAs employ whole words as the unit by concatenating words together inste", "meta": {"name": "Domain Generation Algorithms", "tactics": "command-and-control", "url": "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1568/002", "technique_id": "T1568.002", "is_subtechnique": true}, "similitud": 0.434}, {"texto": "DNS Server. Adversaries may compromise third-party DNS servers that can be used during targeting. During post-compromise activity, adversaries may utilize DNS traffic for various tasks, including for Command and Control (ex: [Application Layer Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071)). Instead of setting up their own DNS servers, adversaries may compromise third-party DNS servers in support of operations.\n\nBy compromising DNS servers, adversaries can alter DNS records. Such control can allow for redirection of an organization's traffic, facilitating Collection and Credential Access efforts for the adversary.(Citation: Talos DNSpionage Nov 2018)(Citation: FireEye DNS Hijack 2019)  Additionally, adversaries may leverage such control in conjunction with [Digital Certificates](http", "meta": {"url": "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/002", "name": "DNS Server", "technique_id": "T1584.002", "is_subtechnique": true, "tactics": "resource-development"}, "similitud": 0.429}]}}
